WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.794
There are two ways to get your bread.

2
00:00:04.794 --> 00:00:10.905
You can walk to the bakery when you want a loaf, or the bakery can leave one on your doorstep every morning.

3
00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:17.591
But go away for two weeks, and you come home to fourteen loaves on the step, the oldest one turning green.

4
00:00:17.591 --> 00:00:21.002
That's not breakfast any more, that's a science project.

5
00:00:21.002 --> 00:00:25.084
The bakery decides how much comes, and nobody asks if you're ready.

6
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.017
Programs move data the same way.

7
00:00:28.017 --> 00:00:35.492
Picture a bakery line: an oven bakes loaves, a belt carries them, and a packer at the end puts each one in a box.

8
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.330
The oven bakes 10,000 loaves a second, and the packer boxes 2,000.

9
00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:45.591
So the extra loaves wait in a queue with no limit, 8,000 more every second.

10
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.901
Each one is an object in memory.

11
00:00:47.901 --> 00:00:51.381
Nothing fails at first, and then the memory runs out.

12
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.993
The lesson's demo tried 100,000 loaves.

13
00:00:54.993 --> 00:01:00.039
The oven was done in 65 milliseconds, and went home happy.

14
00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:03.780
96,807 loaves were still waiting in memory.

15
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.115
What we want is a note on the doorstep.

16
00:01:07.115 --> 00:01:11.177
Here, the packer sends a slip back up the belt: send me 2 more.

17
00:01:11.177 --> 00:01:13.047
The oven sends 2, then waits.

18
00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:20.011
That's called backpressure: the slow side says how much it can take, and the fast side never sends more.

19
00:01:20.233 --> 00:01:23.111
Now a question that's easy to be shy about.

20
00:01:23.111 --> 00:01:27.394
The slip says 2, but the oven has 3 loaves ready, already baked.

21
00:01:27.394 --> 00:01:29.402
Surely the third one goes too?

22
00:01:29.402 --> 00:01:30.607
Hold that thought.

23
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:38.823
A slip only works if both ends read it the same way, and the oven and the packer often come from different libraries.

24
00:01:38.823 --> 00:01:44.823
So they all follow one short contract, called Reactive Streams: four interfaces, seven methods.

25
00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:45.877
Let's map it.

26
00:01:45.877 --> 00:01:50.612
The oven is a Publisher, the source of items, with one method: subscribe.

27
00:01:50.612 --> 00:01:56.255
The packer is a Subscriber, with four methods, one for each thing the oven can tell it.

28
00:01:56.255 --> 00:02:03.065
And the pad of slips between them is a Subscription: request n, send me n more, and cancel, stop sending.

29
00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:10.744
Processor, the fourth, is a station in the middle of the belt: a subscriber on one side, a publisher on the other.

30
00:02:10.744 --> 00:02:16.123
And every method returns void, so each call is a one-way message, called a signal.

31
00:02:16.933 --> 00:02:18.895
Let's watch one conversation.

32
00:02:18.895 --> 00:02:21.533
Today, the oven has three loaves ready.

33
00:02:21.767 --> 00:02:24.184
The packer calls subscribe on the oven.

34
00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.830
The oven answers with onSubscribe, and hands over a pad of slips.

35
00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:29.784
Then, nothing.

36
00:02:29.784 --> 00:02:32.783
The oven owes zero loaves, so not one moves.

37
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.538
The packer writes a slip: request 2.

38
00:02:35.538 --> 00:02:37.301
Now the oven owes it two.

39
00:02:37.533 --> 00:02:39.021
Three loaves are ready.

40
00:02:39.021 --> 00:02:40.832
How many come down the belt?

41
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:44.139
Two.

42
00:02:44.139 --> 00:02:45.413
onNext, loaf-1.

43
00:02:45.413 --> 00:02:46.686
onNext, loaf-2.

44
00:02:46.686 --> 00:02:49.656
Each one uses up one from the slip.

45
00:02:49.656 --> 00:02:53.391
The oven owes zero now, so loaf-3 stays put.

46
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:54.950
That's our answer.

47
00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:56.374
Ready isn't enough.

48
00:02:56.374 --> 00:02:58.399
Only a slip lets a loaf go.

49
00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.060
The packer boxes both and writes another slip for 2.

50
00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:07.715
Only loaf-3 is left, so the oven sends it, and rings the end-of-day bell: onComplete.

51
00:03:07.715 --> 00:03:09.378
Fewer than asked is fine.

52
00:03:09.378 --> 00:03:13.304
If the oven had broken down, it would send onError instead.

53
00:03:13.533 --> 00:03:22.221
So every subscriber hears the same story: one onSubscribe, any number of onNext, then at most one ending, the bell or the breakdown.

54
00:03:22.221 --> 00:03:24.130
The spec writes it like this.

55
00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:28.935
The endings are called terminal signals: after one, nothing else arrives.

56
00:03:29.767 --> 00:03:32.135
The contract has numbered rules.

57
00:03:32.135 --> 00:03:36.576
Rule 1.1: never more onNext signals than requested in total.

58
00:03:36.576 --> 00:03:44.421
Rule 1.3: signals come one at a time, so a subscriber's own fields need no lock to keep other threads out.

59
00:03:44.633 --> 00:03:48.136
Rule 2.13: to stop, a subscriber calls cancel.

60
00:03:48.136 --> 00:03:53.467
It never throws, because the oven's code can't hand an exception back.

61
00:03:53.467 --> 00:03:58.264
And rule 3.9: a request for zero, or less, must end in onError.

62
00:03:59.167 --> 00:04:01.851
Let's break that last rule on purpose.

63
00:04:01.851 --> 00:04:09.622
A packer named Rude writes a slip for zero, once to the JDK's own publisher, and once to Reactor's Flux.range.

64
00:04:09.622 --> 00:04:11.176
What does each one do?

65
00:04:14.167 --> 00:04:18.593
The JDK follows the rule: onError, IllegalArgumentException.

66
00:04:18.593 --> 00:04:22.798
Reactor sends nothing at all: no loaf, no error, no bell.

67
00:04:22.798 --> 00:04:27.520
Just a debug note nobody sees by default, and the stream stalls.

68
00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:31.578
In Reactor, request zero might as well be called wait forever.

69
00:04:31.578 --> 00:04:36.600
So if a stream goes quiet and never ends, look for a slip that was never written.

70
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.335
Here's where the bakery picture breaks.

71
00:04:39.335 --> 00:04:43.691
A real packer who stops writing slips would be missed by lunchtime.

72
00:04:43.691 --> 00:04:47.721
Code has no lunchtime: unmet demand never times out by itself.

73
00:04:48.433 --> 00:04:49.388
One guardrail.

74
00:04:49.388 --> 00:04:57.230
A packer can ask for Long.MAX_VALUE, about nine quintillion loaves, and the oven may read that as: send everything.

75
00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.071
That isn't a slip any more.

76
00:04:59.071 --> 00:05:02.753
That's the doorstep again, with fourteen loaves on it.

77
00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:07.902
You'll rarely write one yourself, because libraries like Reactor do.

78
00:05:07.902 --> 00:05:12.492
But here's a packer in Java and Kotlin that asks for one loaf at a time.

79
00:05:12.492 --> 00:05:14.723
The first slip goes in onSubscribe.

80
00:05:14.723 --> 00:05:18.676
In onNext, it boxes the loaf, and only then asks for the next.

81
00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:23.586
So, does the oven send a loaf just because it's ready?

82
00:05:23.586 --> 00:05:23.813
No.

83
00:05:23.813 --> 00:05:26.688
Demand is the only permission to send.

84
00:05:26.688 --> 00:05:27.975
No slip, no loaf.

85
00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.036
Quick check.

86
00:05:29.036 --> 00:05:33.636
A packer asks for 3 in onSubscribe, and never writes another slip.

87
00:05:33.636 --> 00:05:35.658
The oven has 10 loaves ready.

88
00:05:35.658 --> 00:05:37.400
What does the packer get?

89
00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:42.283
Three loaves, and then nothing.

90
00:05:42.283 --> 00:05:47.847
Not even the bell, because the oven still has loaves to send, and no slip to send them on.
